On Jul 21, 7:03 pm, chuckcar <chuck...@nil.org> wrote:
> My point was, that if you don't know what your doing and the whys of it,
> the last thing you should be doing is compiling and installing software.
> That is never untrue.

If all you want to do is install and play a game on your computer, you
shouldn't have to do anything other than type "install" or its
equivalent. Making the process more complicated not only makes it less
convenient but makes it less reliable. "./configure; make install;"
should be set up to work just fine in simple situations, and most
situations are simple.

I had this exact discussion recently with some product integration
engineers who were trying to grapple with the problem of users wanting
to have their software just run while the original developers were
proud of providing an install system that provided a lot of
flexibility. To the users, who were the *only* people whose opinion
mattered where the product was concerned, that kind of flexibility was
the enemy.



